ed before and/or after treatment because radiation can affect endocrine glands. This is particularly connected to treatments involving the neck region, where side-effect discussions and monitoring needs are explicitly raised. [Data: Entities (4540, 5075); Relationships (9656, 11165)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Suitability constraints and contraindication-like cautions appear for genetic skin cancer syndromes and connective tissue diseases\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The dataset explicitly states that radiation therapy should not be used in people with BASAL CELL NEVUS SYNDROME or XERODERMA PIGMENTOSUM due to increased radiation-related skin cancer risk. This introduces a compliance/safety-like decision gate where patient genetic context can disqualify a modality even if it is otherwise effective. [Data: Entities (651, 652); Relationships (893, 894)]\\n\\nCONNECTIVE TISSUE DISEASES (including lupus and scleroderma) are also identified as situations where radiation therapy may not be appropriate. Although the data does not provide a detailed mechanism, it establishes that comorbidity screening influences modality selection and risk tolerance. [Data: Entities (653, 655); Relationships (909, 910)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Clinical governance emphasizes multidisciplinary decision-making and tradeoffs with surgery/systemic therapy based on residual disease and organ risk\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A MULTIDISCIPLINARY DISCUSSION OF NEXT STEPS among cancer doctors is positioned as a recommended mechanism when concerning features are noted during surgery or when cancer is not fully removed, and radiation therapy is specifically named as something that may be considered based on that discussion. This frames radiation as a key lever in post-operative escalation pathways. [Data: Entities (830, 829, 17); Relationships (1121, 1187)]\\n\\nThe network also describes scenarios where radiation substitutes for surgery (e.g., when someone is a non-surgical 